---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaButtons from '../components/CtaButtons.astro';

// Folder trees are rendered as text via {expressions} so Astro doesn't try to
// parse "<area-slug>" as an HTML element.
const starterTree = `brain/
  current.md
  sources.md
  tasks.md
  log.md`;

const beginnerTree = `brain/
  AGENTS.md
  index.md
  current.md
  log.md
  context/
  projects/
  people/
  sources/
  tasks.md
  memory/`;

const intermediateTree = `brain/
  AGENTS.md
  index.md
  log.md
  shared/
  areas/
    <area-slug>/
      current.md
      projects/
      people/
      sources/
      decisions.md
      tasks.md
      memory.md
  relationships/`;

const proTree = `brain/
  AGENTS.md
  index.md
  log.md
  graph.md
  ontology.md
  areas/
    <area-slug>/
      current.md
      sources/
      decisions/
      tasks/
      memory/
  entities/
    people/
    organizations/
    projects/
    concepts/
  relationships/
    edges.md
    open-conflicts.md
  sources/
    raw/
    processed/
  workflows/
  checks/`;
---

<Base title="Depths" description="Starter, Beginner, Intermediate, Pro — pick how much structure Zo builds today. You can grow later.">
  <h1>Depths</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    You choose how deep to go on the first screen of setup — and there’s no wrong
    answer. The depth just controls how much Zo builds today. Start at Starter and
    grow to Pro whenever you need more.
  </p>

  <div class="next-links">
    <a href="#starter">Starter</a>
    <a href="#beginner">Beginner</a>
    <a href="#intermediate">Intermediate</a>
    <a href="#pro">Pro</a>
  </div>

  <h2 id="starter">Starter — useful in minutes</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Who it’s for:</strong> Anyone who wants the fastest possible first
    brain for one area of work or life. You’re not trying to organize everything —
    you just want AI to stop forgetting the one thing you care about right now.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What Zo asks you:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>What one thing should this brain help with first?</li>
    <li>What should Zo remember?</li>
    <li>What source material do you already have?</li>
    <li>What must Zo never do without your approval?</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>What Zo creates:</strong></p>
  <pre><code>{starterTree}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <strong>How you use it:</strong> Paste or describe one real thing — a meeting
    note, a messy plan, a client situation, a task list. Zo turns it into a
    working summary of what’s true now (<code>current.md</code>), a record of
    where it came from (<code>sources.md</code>), a few next actions
    (<code>tasks.md</code>), and a note of what changed (<code>log.md</code>).
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>Starter is for getting useful in minutes. It isn’t trying to organize your whole life — and that’s exactly why it works.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="beginner">Beginner — enough structure to stop starting cold</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Who it’s for:</strong> You have one to three areas you want AI to
    remember, and you’d like simple folders without learning a knowledge-management
    system.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What Zo creates:</strong></p>
  <pre><code>{beginnerTree}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <strong>How you use it:</strong> Zo builds a basic map of your world and
    separates the obvious things — people, projects, sources, tasks — while
    keeping a lightweight memory of what’s changed. Now you can ask real
    questions: <em>“What matters this week?”</em> or <em>“What did we decide about
    this client?”</em> — and Zo knows where to look instead of guessing.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>Beginner gives you just enough structure for AI to be genuinely helpful, without asking you to become a filing expert first.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="intermediate">Intermediate — where the brain becomes yours</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Who it’s for:</strong> You’ve got multiple parts of life or work that
    need to stay separate but connected. For example: two businesses; client work
    plus family logistics; a job hunt plus creative projects; a health plan plus a
    learning plan; operations plus relationships.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What Zo creates:</strong></p>
  <pre><code>{intermediateTree}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <strong>How you use it:</strong> Zo routes to the right area <em>before</em> it
    answers, so your second business never bleeds into your first. Things that
    span everything live in <code>shared/</code>. The important cross-area
    dependencies — this client belongs to that business, this decision affects
    that project — live in <code>relationships/</code>. Each area keeps its own
    current truth, sources, tasks, decisions, and memory.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>Intermediate is where the brain stops being generic and starts matching your actual life — keeping your businesses, people, and responsibilities separate without losing the connections between them.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="pro">Pro — when the brain becomes infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Who it’s for:</strong> You want a serious personal knowledge system or
    agent workspace — deep continuity, and reasoning that can follow how
    everything connects.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What Zo creates:</strong></p>
  <pre><code>{proTree}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <strong>How you use it:</strong> Zo tracks typed entities (people,
    organizations, projects, concepts), the relationships between them, where each
    fact came from (provenance), open conflicts it hasn’t resolved, validation
    checks, and workflow memory. Instead of re-reading the whole brain, Zo follows
    the graph and routed context — reading only what a question actually needs.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>Pro is for when your brain becomes infrastructure. It supports knowledge-graph behavior and the same routed-context principles as a serious agent workspace — while still being nothing more exotic than local Markdown files you own.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Ready to pick a depth?</h2>
  <CtaButtons note="Zo asks for your depth as the first setup question — and you can change it later." />
</Base>
